Final structured report Overview and objective - The task: Determine the day and month of birth of the author of a referenced article within an African politics/development piece (published 1988–1998) that argues unique historical trajectories in African political growth can be traced by examining shifting patterns. The referenced author later (1992–2001) published a cautionary article arguing that the worldwide spread of democracy would not necessarily bring more stability. - Approach: Identify candidate African articles fitting the description; extract their reference lists; find cited authors who authored cautionary publications between 1992–2001; then retrieve exact day/month of birth for those authors from reliable sources. Present all plausible candidate answers, with evidence, and document uncertainties. Working history and tool use summary - Identified the strongest candidate African article: - Michael Bratton and Nicolas van de Walle, Neopatrimonial Regimes and Political Transitions in Africa. World Politics 46(4), July 1994: 453–489. DOI: 10.2307/2950715 - Evidence fit: Comparative African regime typologies and transition trajectories; abstract explicitly discusses patterned transition dynamics and prospects for democratic consolidation, aligning with “unique historical courses” traced via political growth patterns. - Extracted references: - Cambridge Core publisher page displayed an extensive references section (authors, titles, venues); JSTOR stable page confirmed metadata; Crossref deposit listed 99 references with structured fields and many DOIs. - Key cited authors included: Guillermo O’Donnell (1986 and 1993), Samuel P. Huntington (1991/1992), Juan J. Linz and Alfred Stepan (1978), Adam Przeworski (1986), Richard Snyder (1992), and numerous Africa-focused case studies (e.g., Joel D. Barkan, Marc Pilon, John R. Heilbrunn). - Verified that the “Snyder (1992)” cited is Richard Snyder (Comparative Politics 24(4): 379–399; DOI 10.2307/422151; JSTOR and Brown University profile confirm authorship). Jack Snyder does not appear in the 1994 references. - Identified cautionary publications (1992–2001) by cited authors (arguing democratization does not necessarily yield stability): - Guillermo O’Donnell: Delegative Democracy, Journal of Democracy 5(1), 1994 (DOI 10.1353/jod.1994.0010). Argues weakly institutionalized “delegative” democracies are fragile and unstable. - Samuel P. Huntington: After Twenty Years: The Future of the Third Wave, Journal of Democracy 8(4), 1997. Sober assessment that many third-wave democracies face instability and potential reversals; democratization does not automatically produce order. - Juan J. Linz & Alfred Stepan: Toward Consolidated Democracies, Journal of Democracy 7(2), 1996 (DOI 10.1353/jod.1996.0031). Emphasize necessary institutional/societal conditions for stability; many new democracies fall short. - Adam Przeworski (with coauthors): What Makes Democracies Endure?, Journal of Democracy 7(1), 1996 (DOI 10.1353/jod.1996.0016). Shows stability depends on socioeconomic/institutional factors; democratization alone is insufficient. - Smooha & Hanf (1992), Barkan (1993), Pilon (1993), Heilbrunn (1993): Case-focused cautions that transitions can be stalled or yield fragile outcomes; democratic forms may not produce stability in deeply divided societies or flawed transitions. - Retrieved birth day/month for candidate authors using reliable sources: - Guillermo O’Donnell — Wikipedia confirms February 24, 1936. - Samuel P. Huntington — Wikipedia confirms April 18, 1927; corroborated by NYT obituary. - Juan J. Linz — Wikipedia confirms December 24, 1926. - Alfred Stepan — Wikipedia confirms July 22, 1936. - Adam Przeworski — Wikipedia confirms May 5, 1940; corroborated by British Academy. - Theodor Hanf — German Wikipedia confirms March 20, 1936. - Joel D. Barkan — Wikipedia confirms April 28, 1941; corroborated by University of Iowa memorial and Washington Post obituary; LoC authority lists death date and birthplace. - John R. Heilbrunn — Library of Congress authority record explicitly states “b. 6/26/54”. - Sammy Smooha — Wikipedia, LoC, and VIAF list year only (1941); day and month not publicly available in checked authorities. - Marc Pilon — LoC authority lists year only (1953); day and month not publicly available in checked authorities. - Richard Snyder — Brown University profile does not list DOB; day and month not publicly available in checked sources. Detailed evidence and links - 1994 African article: - Cambridge Core page (references visible): https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/world-politics/article/abs/neopatrimonial-regimes-and-political-transitions-in-africa/7F37E817AE25E0E7CADC135AD946E6DD - JSTOR stable page: https://www.jstor.org/stable/2950715 - Crossref record (99 references): https://api.crossref.org/works/10.2307/2950715 - Key references cited by the 1994 article and cautionary publications (1992–2001): - O’Donnell (1986; cited) → Delegative Democracy (1994): DOI 10.1353/jod.1994.0010; Project MUSE: https://muse.jhu.edu/article/225426 - Huntington (PSQ 106(4), cited as 1992; Crossref shows Dec. 1991): DOI 10.2307/2151795; OUP: https://academic.oup.com/psq/article/106/4/579/7135332 → After Twenty Years (1997) - Linz & Stepan (1978; cited) → Toward Consolidated Democracies (1996): DOI 10.1353/jod.1996.0031; https://muse.jhu.edu/article/16745 - Przeworski (1986; cited) → What Makes Democracies Endure? (1996): DOI 10.1353/jod.1996.0016 - Smooha & Hanf (1992): DOI 10.1177/002071529203300103 - Barkan (1993): DOI 10.1353/jod.1993.0045 - Pilon (1993): DOI 10.3406/polaf.1993.5648 - Heilbrunn (1993): DOI 10.1017/S0022278X00011939 - Richard Snyder (1992; cited): JSTOR: https://www.jstor.org/stable/422151; Brown profile: https://vivo.brown.edu/display/rsnyder - Birth date sources: - Guillermo O’Donnell: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillermo_O%27Donnell - Adam Przeworski: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_Przeworski; https://www.thebritishacademy.ac.uk/fellows/professor-adam-przeworski-fba/ - Samuel P. Huntington: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_P._Huntington; NYT obituary: https://www.nytimes.com/2008/12/29/education/29huntington.html - Juan J. Linz: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Linz - Alfred Stepan: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Stepan - Theodor Hanf: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodor_Hanf - Joel D. Barkan: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joel_Barkan; University of Iowa memorial: http://clas.uiowa.edu/faculty/joel-d-barkan-1941-2014; Washington Post obituary: https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/obituaries/joel-d-barkan-professor-and-scholar/2014/02/03/b197be5c-8d06-11e3-95dd-36ff657a4dae_story.html; LoC authority: https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79063655.html - John R. Heilbrunn: LoC authority: https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no95056516.html (Sources note: “b. 6/26/54”) - Sammy Smooha: Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sammy_Smooha; LoC: https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n78055583.html; VIAF: https://viaf.org/en/viaf/54193971 - Marc Pilon: LoC authority: https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n2006056408.html; VIAF link: https://viaf.org/en/viaf/LC%7Cn%202006056408 - Richard Snyder: Brown profile: https://vivo.brown.edu/display/rsnyder (DOB not listed) Identity and exclusion checks - The “Snyder (1992)” cited in Bratton & van de Walle (1994) is Richard Snyder, Comparative Politics 24(4), 1992, DOI 10.2307/422151. Verified via JSTOR and Brown University profile. - Jack Snyder (with Mansfield) authored iconic cautionary articles in 1995, but Jack Snyder is not cited in Bratton & van de Walle (1994). Therefore, he is not the “author of the referenced article” in this chain. Uncertainties, conflicts, and alternative interpretations - The specific phrasing “spread of democracy worldwide wouldn’t necessarily bring more stability” most closely evokes Mansfield & Snyder (1995) and Zakaria (1997). However, within the verified reference list of the 1994 African article, the strongest matching cited authors with later cautionary publications are O’Donnell (1994), Huntington (1997), Linz & Stepan (1996), and Przeworski et al. (1996). - Huntington’s “How Countries Democratize” appears in Dec. 1991 but is cited as 1992 in the 1994 article; his 1997 Journal of Democracy piece clearly falls within the 1992–2001 window and matches the cautionary theme. - Some cited authors (Smooha, Pilon, Barkan, Heilbrunn) present case-specific cautions; while they warn that democratization can stall or be fragile, their focus is narrower than the global thesis. Birth dates for Smooha and Pilon were only available as years; Richard Snyder’s DOB was not publicly available in sources checked. Candidate answers (day and month) aligned to the chain - Strongest global-fit candidates: - Guillermo O’Donnell — February 24 - Samuel P. Huntington — April 18 - Juan J. Linz — December 24 - Alfred Stepan — July 22 - Adam Przeworski — May 5 - Narrower/contextual-fit candidates from cited references: - Theodor Hanf — March 20 - Joel D. Barkan — April 28 - Not available (exact day/month not found in authority sources checked): - Sammy Smooha — 1941 (year only) - Marc Pilon — 1953 (year only) - Richard Snyder — not publicly listed (institutional profile omits DOB) Final reasoning and selection - The most straightforward interpretation, grounded in verifiable references and a later cautionary publication squarely within 1992–2001, points to Guillermo O’Donnell as the author of a referenced work (Transitions from Authoritarian Rule, 1986) who later published Delegative Democracy (1994), arguing that democratization does not necessarily yield stability. His birth day and month are well-documented. Final answer \boxed{February 24}
---
LLM extracted final answer: **Step-by-step Analysis:** 1. The African‐focused article that fits the 1988-1998 window and argues that distinct historical trajectories can be traced through changing patterns of political development is Michael Bratton & Nicolas van de Walle, “Neopatrimonial Regimes and Political Transitions in Africa” (World Politics, July 1994). 2. Among the works cited in that 1994 article is Guillermo O’Donnell’s 1986 study on transitions from authoritarian rule. 3. Guillermo O’Donnell subsequently published “Delegative Democracy” in 1994 (within the 1992-2001 window), contending that the worldwide spread of democracy would not automatically yield greater stability, because many new democracies remain weakly institutionalised and thus fragile. 4. Therefore, the “author of the referenced article” is Guillermo O’Donnell. 5. Reliable biographical sources (e.g., Wikipedia) state that Guillermo O’Donnell was born on 24 February 1936. 6. The question asks specifically for the day and month of his birth. **Final Answer:** \boxed{February 24} **Confidence:** 72 **Supporting Evidence:** • Bratton & van de Walle (1994) reference O’Donnell (1986). • O’Donnell’s “Delegative Democracy” (Journal of Democracy, 1994) argues democratization does not ensure stability. • Multiple authoritative biographies list O’Donnell’s birthdate as 24 February 1936. **Potential Weaknesses:** • Other cited authors (e.g., Samuel P. Huntington, Adam Przeworski) also published cautionary pieces on democracy’s stability between 1992-2001; if the intended author were one of them, the birth date would differ.
